/**
 * components/site/intro/LogoReveal.tsx — the brand mark, as geometry, and the
 * one gesture the intro exists to show.
 *
 * The timing, the paint and the legibility argument are in
 * ./logo-reveal.module.css. THIS FILE IS ABOUT WHERE THE SHAPES CAME FROM.
 *
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * ── PROVENANCE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * THE GEOMETRY BELOW IS GENERATED. It is traced from
 * `public/brand/logo-source.png` (2046x769, RGBA, straight alpha) by
 * `scripts/gen-logo-paths.mjs`, which owns exactly one fenced block in this
 * file and writes nothing outside it.
 *
 *     node scripts/gen-logo-paths.mjs            retrace and write
 *     node scripts/gen-logo-paths.mjs --check    fail if the mark has drifted
 *
 * THAT IS THE POINT OF THIS ROUND, AND IT MATTERED MORE THAN THE SHAPES. The
 * paths that shipped here before were produced by a one-off process that no
 * longer existed, and this comment used to say "IF THE VECTOR ORIGINAL
 * SURFACES, RE-RUN THE TRACE" about a trace nothing in the repository could
 * re-run. So when the owner reported that the N of NGUYEN looked smudged after
 * the animation settled, there was no way to fix it except by hand-editing a
 * cubic — which would have left the real defect in place.
 *
 * ⚠ HE WAS RIGHT, AND HERE IS WHAT IT WAS. The first N's UPPER-RIGHT COUNTER
 * WAS FILLED IN. Its outline ran straight across the top from the left serif to
 * the right stem, so the triangle between the diagonal and the right stem was
 * enclosed by the contour and `fill-rule: evenodd` painted it solid. Measured
 * on that glyph alone, against the source alpha:
 *
 *                            old trace        now
 *     max inscribed disc     41.6 units       18.4   (source 18.4)
 *     ink area               +43.7%           +0.7%
 *     5th-pct row run        2.23 px          4.62   (source 4.70)
 *     thin/thick ratio       23.8             5.3    (source 5.1)
 *
 * THE CAUSE WAS THE SIMPLIFICATION PASS, not the curve fit and not the corner
 * threshold that first looked guilty. The old trace decimated its contours with
 * Ramer-Douglas-Peucker at 0.35 px before fitting them, and RDP keeps the points
 * FAR FROM THE CHORD of a run — which is exactly the wrong criterion at a
 * hairpin, where a counter's outline turns back on itself. The tip's own points
 * are the first thing discarded. The corner detector then reads a polyline in
 * which the corner is no longer present, misses it at any threshold, and hands
 * the fitter both sides as one smooth run; the least-squares cubic through a
 * hairpin is the chord across its mouth. So this trace does no simplification at
 * all before fitting, and pays 5,700 bytes for it. The generator's header
 * carries the isolation: 58-degree corner detection WITHOUT decimation costs two
 * hairline warnings and no topology at all.
 *
 * WHOLE MARK, both traces rendered by the same code into the same box at the
 * source's own resolution, each glyph scored alone:
 *
 *     old trace     added 8.48%   removed 7.44%
 *     now           added 0.64%   removed 0.79%
 *
 * NOTHING HERE DEPENDS ON THE PNG AT RUNTIME. Every path is baked into the
 * module, so the component renders identically with the PNG deleted: no fetch,
 * no decode, no 404, no layout shift. The source is a build-time input.
 *
 * THE SOURCE IS A RASTER, NOT A VECTOR, and one thing in the mark is genuinely
 * below its resolution: the fragment of the D's bowl that the flourish cuts off
 * tapers to a tip ONE SOURCE PIXEL WIDE, which no half-coverage level set can
 * carry at full width. It is 0.65% of the mark's ink at 0.32 CSS px per source
 * pixel, it is named in `logo-trace.json` under `subPixel`, and the alternative
 * — thresholding on a local peak instead of the artwork's plateau — would
 * dilate every stroke in the mark to recover it. If the vector original ever
 * surfaces, point the generator at it; nothing else in the feature changes,
 * because the grouping, the pen strokes and the timing are keyed to the run
 * names and not to the point data.
 *
 * ── HOW THE MARK WAS DECOMPOSED, AND THE THING THAT FOUND ─────────────────
 *
 * Pixels were split into a cream layer and a crimson layer by hue, then
 * 8-connected components were taken per layer. The component map is the whole
 * design, so it is worth stating what it returned:
 *
 *     cream     12 components — D, N, one 15x48 sliver, and 9 wordmark glyphs
 *     crimson   12 components — TWO pieces of one flourish, and 10 tagline
 *                               glyphs
 *
 * THE FLOURISH IS ONE STROKE THAT ARRIVES AS TWO COMPONENTS, AND THE D
 * ARRIVES WITH A PIECE MISSING. Those two facts are the same fact, and they
 * are the mark:
 *
 *     the crimson flourish is cut in half at y 172-186 by the cream N
 *         -> the N is painted OVER the flourish
 *     the D's bowl is cut at x 229-244, y 132-180 by the flourish
 *         -> the flourish is painted OVER the D
 *
 * So the painting order is forced: D, then flourish, then N. The crimson
 * crosses over the D and disappears under the N. That interlock is not a
 * decoration on the mark, it IS the mark, and it is the reason the reveal
 * draws the flourish while the N is still resolving instead of afterwards.
 * (The brief described the crossing element as a crimson N over a cream D.
 * The artwork has a cream D AND a cream N, crossed by a separate crimson
 * calligraphic flourish. Same gesture, different parts; the geometry is what
 * is implemented.)
 *
 * ── THE TRACE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Marching squares at 50% of the artwork's own opaque plateau (alpha 253, so
 * the level is 126.5) over the antialiased alpha channel, so the contours are
 * sub-pixel and follow the artwork's edge rather than the pixel grid. Contours
 * are chained DIRECTED, on integer grid-edge ids, with inside always on the
 * left — the old note here said a directed walk "silently drops whole glyphs",
 * which was true of the table it had rather than of directed walks, and the
 * undirected chain it fell back to is the other half of how one N lost its
 * outline. Then Schneider least-squares cubic fitting at 0.30 px with corner
 * detection at 36 degrees, and NO simplification pass before it.
 *
 * The tolerance is 0.30 rather than tighter because of the only test that
 * settles it: rendering the source and the candidates AT THE SIZE THE MARK IS
 * PAINTED, 0.15 px and 0.30 px are the same picture, and 0.15 costs 4.0 KB
 * gzipped on this page's server-rendered HTML.
 *
 * Holes fall out for free under `fill-rule: evenodd`, which is why every path
 * below carries it.
 *
 * 38.0 KB of path data for the entire mark — 5.7 KB more than the same trace
 * with a lossy simplification pass, which is what that pass costs in bytes and
 * what it was costing in fidelity.
 *
 * Every counter, every hairline, the thick/thin ratio of this high-contrast
 * serif, and every corner is gated PER GLYPH on the rendered result by the
 * generator; `logo-trace.json` publishes the per-glyph numbers.
 *
 * ── THE PEN STROKES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The reveal draws the monogram, so it needs centrelines, which a fill trace
 * does not produce. The flourish's was computed — the mean x of its crimson
 * pixels per scanline, smoothed, then fitted — and the maximum perpendicular
 * distance from that centreline to any flourish pixel is 18.3 units, so a pen
 * 46 wide covers it with margin.
 *
 * The D's and the N's are hand-authored from the letterforms' own per-row
 * run structure, and each was checked against every sampled row:
 *
 *     stroke                 pen   covers                       widest run
 *     D  stem + foot          62   x 5-67 down, then the foot   26 / 129
 *     D  top arm + bowl       54   arm x 2-155, bowl to y 196   40
 *     N  left stem            50   x 118-168                    32
 *     N  diagonal             46   x 154-200 at y132 ...        33
 *                                  ... 290-337 at y286
 *     N  right stem           46   x 300-346                    25
 *     FL flourish             46   computed centreline          17
 *
 * These are approximations and they are allowed to be, because the masks in
 * logo-reveal.module.css also carry a full-bleed coverage rect that closes
 * behind the pen. The pen owns the motion; the rect owns the correctness. See
 * THE COVERAGE GUARANTEE there.
 *
 * THEY ARE NOT REGENERATED BY THE TRACER, BUT THEY ARE CHECKED BY IT. A retrace
 * moves the outlines under a hand-authored centreline, so the generator strokes
 * each run's pens at the widths declared below and measures what fraction of
 * that run's ink they still cover. On the current trace: D 100.0%, N 99.7%,
 * flourish 100.0%. It reports rather than fails, for the reason above — but a
 * retrace that walked the geometry out from under a pen shows up as a number
 * falling instead of as a shrug in review.
 *
 * ── COORDINATES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The source's ink box is x 156-1884, y 221-527. Everything below is
 * translated by (-150, -216) and rounded to 0.1, which at the ~560 CSS px the
 * mark occupies at a 1280 viewport is a rounding error of 0.03 px. The
 * viewBox is inset by a further 10 units on every side so the halo stroke
 * cannot clip against the edge.
 *
 * There is also a HALF PIXEL in that transform, and it is not a fudge. The
 * alpha grid is sampled at pixel CENTRES, while SVG user space puts pixel
 * (x,y) on the square [x,x+1]x[y,y+1]. Leave it out and the whole mark lands up
 * and left by half a source pixel — which reads as a fidelity problem rather
 * than a registration one: the tagline E measured +23.0%/-13.8% of its ink
 * without it and +0.0%/-0.8% with it.
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 */

import type { CSSProperties } from "react";

import s from "./logo-reveal.module.css";

/**
 * The mark's own running time, for whoever schedules the dissolve. The last
 * tagline glyph lands at 2.50s and the mark then holds still for 0.10s, so a
 * cut taken at or after LOGO_REVEAL_MS is guaranteed a static frame.
 *
 * Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the mark is composed and at rest the
 * instant it renders — LOGO_REVEAL_REDUCED_MS is 0, not a shortened sequence.
 * There is no drawing, no movement and no fade to wait for, so the shell may
 * cut whenever it likes without ever catching the mark half-formed. (It had a
 * 0.4s fade here once; the shell's own reduced-motion path takes the overlay
 * down by ~150ms, so the mark was invisible for its entire life. A resting
 * state that needs the shell to wait is not a resting state.)
 */
export const LOGO_REVEAL_MS = 2600;
export const LOGO_REVEAL_REDUCED_MS = 0;

/**
 * Every href known to carry the artwork these paths were traced from.
 *
 * `app/page.tsx` hands this component the href its gate actually opened on,
 * and the two can drift: if a vector export ever lands at
 * `/brand/logo-source.svg`, `INTRO_LOGO_CANDIDATES` prefers it, the gate turns
 * the intro on for THAT file, and this module keeps drawing the old raster
 * trace — a silent, correct-looking staleness.
 *
 * TWO ENTRIES, NOT ONE, BECAUSE THEY ARE THE SAME BYTES. The owner dropped the
 * lockup at `personal_brand.png`; it was then also published under the
 * canonical `logo-source.png`. Verified identical 2026-09-03:
 *
 *     md5  a93bc6d70c35cf0b652f995489fc43f1   228,158 bytes   2046 x 769
 *
 * for BOTH files. The trace is valid for whichever one the gate happens to
 * prefer, and flagging the alias as drift would be a false alarm — which is
 * how a gate gets trained out of people. If they ever stop being identical,
 * one of these entries is a lie and the md5 above is how to find out which.
 *
 * The href is otherwise never fetched. It is compared, published on the
 * element as `data-src` / `data-stale`, and warned about. There is
 * deliberately no runtime fallback to drawing the passed file: an <img> of the
 * lockup cannot be revealed stroke by stroke, and substituting a still image
 * on drift would hide exactly the thing that needs fixing.
 */
export const TRACED_SOURCES: readonly string[] = [
  "/brand/logo-source.png",
  "/brand/personal_brand.png",
];

/* ── BEGIN GENERATED GEOMETRY — scripts/gen-logo-paths.mjs ─────────────── */
